Umm, no, he was the first Rider I encountered in the Astral Plane.

I think the lesson I'm taking away from this (well, one of the lessons)
is that for the late game I need to ensure I have a reliable method to
cure sickness, ideally the spell.  My now #2 high score from a few
months back was a wizard killed in Orcus-town by Demogorgon.

One of the little annoying details of this game was that I had learned
cure sickness and could cast it with 0% failure, but had forgotten it by
the time I reached Astral.  As I discovered when I tried casting it.
And the unicorn horn wasn't working fast enough, and I think by then I
was out of potions of full healing (I used up most of them trying to
survive the air elementals in the Plane of Air).  I had a whole lot of
wands, including slow monster, which I probably should have used more of
(I could spare breaking one of the low-charge wands of death to clear
out some space around me shortly into Astral, and save mana points for
casting finger of death).  And maybe I should have worked on getting
basic skill for Frost Brand; I had it but I had stuck with using
Magicbane and a stack of +6 elven daggers as my main weapon attacks.

One small satisfaction was that I sent screenshots of the finale to a
friend who is insisting on remaining unspoiled in NetHack, but gave me
permission to at least tell him about my more interesting games without
having to engage in full spoiler protection.  His response?  "There's an
_Astral Plane_?"  The best game he's ever told me about was one where
his 30th level wizard _left the dungeon_, looking better-equipped than
mine was.  He apparently hasn't picked up the clues about the real
endgame yet.

It was also satisfying, whenever Rodney kept showing up with "So thou
thought thou couldst defeat me blah blah blah", to respond with "Eat
finger of death, Rodney!"  Other than the double trouble when I first
took him on, every other meeting (12 in total?) was no trouble at all,
just instant Rodney corpse.

